DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  sseettnnss() system call allows the calling thread to move into differ‐
       ent namespaces.  The _f_d argument is one of the following:

       •  a file  descriptor  referring  to  one  of  the  magic  links  in  a
          _/_p_r_o_c_/pid_/_n_s_/ directory (or a bind mount to such a link);

       •  a PID file descriptor (see ppiiddffdd__ooppeenn(2)).

       The _n_s_t_y_p_e argument is interpreted differently in each case.

   ffdd rreeffeerrss ttoo aa _//_pp_rr_oo_cc_//ppiidd_//_nn_ss_// lliinnkk
       If  _f_d  refers  to  a _/_p_r_o_c_/pid_/_n_s_/ link, then sseettnnss() reassociates the
       calling thread with the namespace associated with that link, subject to
       any constraints imposed by the _n_s_t_y_p_e argument.  In  this  usage,  each
       call to sseettnnss() changes just one of the caller’s namespace memberships.

       The  _n_s_t_y_p_e  argument  specifies  which  type  of namespace the calling
       thread may be reassociated with.  This argument can  have  _o_n_e  of  the
       following values:

       00      Allow any type of namespace to be joined.

       CCLLOONNEE__NNEEWWCCGGRROOUUPP (since Linux 4.6)
              _f_d must refer to a cgroup namespace.

       CCLLOONNEE__NNEEWWIIPPCC (since Linux 3.0)
              _f_d must refer to an IPC namespace.

       CCLLOONNEE__NNEEWWNNEETT (since Linux 3.0)
              _f_d must refer to a network namespace.

       CCLLOONNEE__NNEEWWNNSS (since Linux 3.8)
              _f_d must refer to a mount namespace.

       CCLLOONNEE__NNEEWWPPIIDD (since Linux 3.8)
              _f_d must refer to a descendant PID namespace.

       CCLLOONNEE__NNEEWWTTIIMMEE (since Linux 5.8)
              _f_d must refer to a time namespace.

       CCLLOONNEE__NNEEWWUUSSEERR (since Linux 3.8)
              _f_d must refer to a user namespace.

       CCLLOONNEE__NNEEWWUUTTSS (since Linux 3.0)
              _f_d must refer to a UTS namespace.

       Specifying  _n_s_t_y_p_e as 0 suffices if the caller knows (or does not care)
       what type of namespace is referred to  by  _f_d.   Specifying  a  nonzero
       value  for  _n_s_t_y_p_e  is  useful if the caller does not know what type of
       namespace is referred to by _f_d and wants to ensure that  the  namespace
       is  of  a  particular type.  (The caller might not know the type of the
       namespace referred to by _f_d if the file descriptor was  opened  by  an‐
       other  process and, for example, passed to the caller via a UNIX domain
       socket.)

